her life  she'll be going through a series of  tests to understand  how she became a long hauler do you feel  like a guinea pig  i do a bit because we don't we just  don't know  how this is going to play out yet every  time mclean visits  she'll be getting an ultrasound and ecg  done on her heart  a ct scan of her lungs  pulmonary test to see how her lungs are  handling the weight of it all  how exhausting is all of that on top of  having covert  from being a person who pre-covered you  know would  be going to work and then yoga classes  and seeing friends and and now i'm i  really have to  plan the day to kind of conserve the  energy i have  so it's hard yeah it's exhausting so  what could be causing the extreme  exhaustion  really we don't we don't know the answer  at this stage that's because the  long-haul condition  is so new it's impossible to know how  many people could be in this category  but there's a growing number of patients  all over the world  who say they have long coveted i always  knew it was covid and i was told very  early on it was coped  hannah davis believes she got coveted in  march  at the time she lived in new york city  the epicenter of the virus in the united  states early  on the hospitals were completely  overflowing  they really were trying to keep us away  from the hospital they explicitly said  if you're out of breath while you're  walking across your room  that's not good enough to come into the  hospital when davis finally did get  tested it was  31 days after the onset of her symptoms  when i got tested the doctor said  this isn't really worth doing after 14  days these tests are worthless  this is going to come back negative and  it did davis is now part of a  long-hauler support group  many of whom say they were infected but  told to stay home  so they never had a positive test the  more common experience is that you get a  negative test and your doctor says oh it  wasn't covid  it must be something else and then  you're on not 