ered  i feel like i'm recovering however i  still feel the  impact of the virus in my body and i'm  not back to my baseline  i still feel permanently changed it's  the reason robinson has teamed up with  the postcovid clinic at st  paul's both as a test subject and a  nurse working in the clinic  so she can see this disease from the  lens of a medical professional  and a patient the thing that's  interesting about my case is they don't  understand why some  people under the age of 50 with no other  chronic health conditions  could get such a severe impact like  severe version of this virus  i can tell you that i'm seeing a lot of  people under the age of 50  that are having sort of ongoing symptoms  i'm seeing people over the age of 50 as  well  coincidentally both robinson and maclean  are the same age  42 for katie mclean she's focused on  getting better  she hopes the testing will one day help  her escape the impact of covid  and the waves of exhaustion that rule  her life  it's going to be a long haul for the  research as well are you prepared for  that  i think advocating about it is really  important and being a part of the  research is really important  to help other people you know and  hopefully  the more we know less people will have  to go through this  and have their lives kind of severely  turned upside down by  by this infection  next week on the new reality i didn't  really feel  like human you make money so that you  can live  but i wasn't making enough money to live  what a basic income does is  provides a foundation from which  people can grow you see entrepreneurship  you see employment you see people doing  better in their lives overall  to have the capacity to live the human  experience and  have relationships and have a healthy  lifestyle  and it's really hard to do that when  you're poor every canadian deserves to  live in dignity and a wealthy country  like  ours we can afford to pay for  and that is the new reality for this  week thanks for 